# FlowDocs Frontend Architecture

This is a workflow-automation / document-processing platform (FlowDocs) with a
node-based visual editor, similar in spirit to n8n or Zapier's flow builder.

## Folder structure (do not deviate without asking)

- `src/components/` — all UI components, grouped by feature:
  - `Sidebar/` — left nav (CategorySection.tsx, ComponentSidebar.tsx)
  - `Toolbar/` — top toolbar (TopToolbar.tsx)
  - `WorkflowManager/` — the node canvas editor (WorkflowManager.tsx)
  - `Nodes/` — individual node components rendered on the canvas
  - `ExecutionPanel/` — run/execution view with live logs and node status
- `src/config/nodeDefinitions.ts` — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for all node types,
  their categories (Preprocessing, OCR, RAG/Embeddings, Extraction,
  Export/Output, Logic/Conditions), icons, and default parameters. Never
  hardcode node type lists elsewhere — always import from here.
- `src/engine/workflowEngine.ts` — execution logic (running a workflow,
  resolving node order via DAG, handling node status transitions). UI
  components must NOT duplicate this logic; they call into the engine.
- `src/store/` — Zustand stores, one concern each:
  - `flowStore.ts` — canvas state: nodes, edges, selected node, zoom/pan
  - `workflowStore.ts` — workflow CRUD: list of workflows, metadata, save/load
  - `executionStore.ts` — run state: current run, per-node status, logs
  Do not merge these stores or put canvas state in workflowStore, etc.
- `src/types/index.ts` — ALL shared TypeScript types/interfaces live here
  (Node, Edge, Workflow, ExecutionRun, NodeStatus, etc). No inline duplicate
  type definitions in components.
- `src/utils/dagUtils.ts` — graph helpers (topological sort, cycle detection,
  finding upstream/downstream nodes). Reuse these; don't reimplement graph
  logic inside components.
- `src/utils/idGenerator.ts` — use this for all new node/edge/workflow IDs.

## Component conventions

- Functional components only, with hooks. No class components.
- TypeScript strict mode — no `any` unless explicitly justified in a comment.
- Each component folder can contain a matching `.tsx` file and co-located
  small subcomponents if needed, but shared logic goes in `utils/` or
  `engine/`, not duplicated across components.
- State: local UI state (e.g. "is dropdown open") can use `useState`.
  Anything that needs to persist or be shared across components (workflow
  data, execution state, canvas state) MUST go through the appropriate
  Zustand store — never lift shared state into React context ad hoc.

## Key domain concepts (for correct component behavior)

- A **Workflow** is a DAG of **Nodes** connected by **Edges**.
- Nodes belong to categories (Preprocessing, OCR, RAG/Embeddings, Extraction,
  Export/Output, Logic/Conditions) — each category has a distinct accent
  color used for the node's left border / icon (see design-system.mdc).
- Nodes have a status at any time: `idle`, `running`, `success`, `error`,
  `ready`. This status drives both the node's badge in the canvas and its
  row in the ExecutionPanel logs.
- A **Run** is an execution of a workflow against one or more input
  documents; it has an overall progress percentage and per-node status/logs.

## When building a new screen or component

1. Check `nodeDefinitions.ts` and `types/index.ts` first — reuse existing
   types/definitions rather than inventing new shapes.
2. Check whether the needed state already exists in one of the three stores
   before adding new state.
3. Keep execution-related UI (ExecutionPanel) read-only / driven by
   `executionStore` — it should never mutate `flowStore` directly.
